What is a Proxy Server?

A proxy server sits between a client and a backend server.

It forwards requests from the client → backend server.

Types:

Forward Proxy → client → proxy → internet (used for filtering, caching, anonymity).

Reverse Proxy → client → proxy → backend servers (used for load balancing, caching, SSL termination).

🔹 Proxy Server as a Load Balancer (Reverse Proxy)

When used as a reverse proxy, the proxy server can act as a load balancer by distributing incoming traffic across multiple backend servers.

How it works:

Clients send requests to the proxy server.

Proxy decides which backend server should handle the request.

Distributes requests based on algorithms like:

Round Robin (even distribution)

Least Connections (send to server with fewer active connections)

IP Hash (same client always hits same server)

Sends the response back to the client.

🔹 Example Setup
          Client
            |
            v
   +-------------------+
   |   Proxy Server    |  ← acts as reverse proxy + load balancer
   | (NGINX / HAProxy) |
   +-------------------+
      |        |        |
      v        v        v
  Server 1   Server 2  Server 3


The client thinks it’s only talking to the proxy.

The proxy balances load across backend servers.

🔹 Real-world Tools

NGINX (reverse proxy + load balancer)

HAProxy (high-performance load balancer)

Envoy (modern proxy, used in service meshes like Istio)

Apache HTTP Server (can also load balance with modules)

🔹 Benefits

Scalability → distribute traffic across multiple servers.

High Availability → if one server fails, proxy reroutes traffic.

Security → hides backend servers’ IPs, central SSL termination.

Performance → can cache static content, compress responses.

✅ In short:
A proxy server becomes a load balancer when it doesn’t just forward traffic, but also intelligently distributes requests across multiple backend servers.

